# Meet Me at Zero

## Chapter 1: The Bottle Came Back

Clink. Clink. Clink.

Something was knocking on the bottom step of the lighthouse.

Tully thundered down the stairs, round and round and round, and flung open the front door. The sea was sloshing against the bottom step, the way it always did. And bonking against the step, clink, clink, was a bottle.

Not just any bottle. Tully's bottle! A week ago, he'd squashed a letter inside it, pushed in the cork and thrown it as far into the sea as he could. The letter said: *Hello! I'm Tully. I live at Number Three. Will you be my friend?*

Now the bottle was back. And it was clearing its throat.

"Ahem," said the bottle. "Special delivery."

Tully sat down with a bump. "You can *talk*?"

"Of course I can talk. I'm a message bottle. First class. Corker's the name, and I have sailed the seven seas." The bottle paused. "Well. This sea. Seven times."

Inside Corker was a brand-new letter. It was soggy and curly, it smelled of salt, and it was written in wiggly purple-black ink.

*Dear Tully, I live at Number Three too! I've never met ANYBODY else who lives at Number Three. Let's meet! Come to Number Three tomorrow at sunset. Love from Octavia, Number Three.*

Tully frowned. He knew exactly where Number Three was. The lighthouse had eight floors, stacked up like building blocks. Floor One was the kitchen. Floor Two was Mum's room. Floor Three was Tully's room. Up and up the floors went, all the way to Floor Eight, where the great lamp turned round and round all night.

And at the very bottom was the front step, where Mum had painted a big white zero.

"Zero's the bottom, love," Mum always said. "Nothing's lower than zero. Everybody knows that."

So Octavia must be coming *here*. To his room!

Tully scribbled *YES PLEASE!* on a scrap of paper, popped it inside Corker and threw him back into the waves.

A pelican swooped down. SCOOP! Corker vanished into its enormous beak.

"Mmmf!" said the beak.

The pelican gulped. It frowned. Then, PTOOEY! Out shot Corker, and he landed in the sea with a plop.

"Meant to do that," said Corker, bobbing away. "I'm a professional."

The next day, Tully tidied his room. He put on his party hat. At sunset, he sat on his bed on Floor Three and waited.

And waited.

Nobody came. Only a seagull, who looked at his party hat, said "Ark," and flew off.

But here's something Tully didn't know. At that very moment, far below his feet, below the kitchen, below the bottom step, down under the green, swishing sea, somebody else was waiting at Number Three, too.

She had eight arms. And every single one of them was holding a party streamer.

## Chapter 2: Two Number Threes

Clink. Clink. CLINK.

Corker was back, and the letter inside him was very cross.

*Dear Tully, oh my eight arms, WHERE WERE YOU? I waited at Number Three until my arms went wrinkly! From Octavia, Number Three.*

"But I *was* at Number Three!" said Tully. He grabbed a pencil.

*I WAS at Number Three! I waited till the stars came out! From Tully, ALSO Number Three.*

Then he had an idea. *Let's try somewhere else,* he wrote. *Meet me at Number Five.*

Floor Five was the library, with dusty books and one very squishy armchair.

"Be careful this time," Tully told Corker, and threw him into the sea.

Corker was very careful. He was so careful that he didn't notice a seagull landing on top of him. She fluffed up her feathers and settled down, as if he were an egg.

"Madam," said Corker, "I am *not* an egg. I am a professional."

"Ark," said the seagull, and stayed exactly where she was.

At sunset, Tully sat in the squishy armchair on Floor Five and waited.

Nobody came.

Next morning, Corker clinked in with seaweed on his cork and another letter. *I waited at Number Five,* it said, *right beside old Mr Groper, who SNORES. Where were you THIS time?*

Tully held the letter up to the window, the way a scientist would. It was soggy. It smelled of the sea. In every corner were little round marks, as if somebody had held it with... suckers.

And a groper! Tully knew about gropers. They were giant spotty fish that lived around the rocks. Under the water.

Tully stood on one leg. He always stood on one leg when he was thinking hard. Nobody knew why. Not even Tully.

"What if," he said, "Octavia lives *inside* the lighthouse? In a secret room?"

He tapped every wall on Floor Three. Knock, knock, knock. He found a spider, a lost jigsaw piece and one of Mum's socks.

"What are you doing, love?" asked Mum.

"Looking for the other Number Three."

"But this is the only Number Three, love," said Mum, "you're standing in it."

Tully swapped legs, poked his head out the window and called down, "Corker, when you deliver my letters, who takes them out of you?"

"No idea," Corker called back up. "Something tickles my bottom, flips me round about, and when I look, the letter's gone. Very mysterious."

Something tickled him. From *underneath*.

"What if," said Tully slowly, "Octavia lives *under the water*?"

Mum laughed. "Under the water? That's *below zero*, love. There's nothing below zero. Zero's the bottom!"

"Quite right," said Corker. "I've been to zero a thousand times. I float there. There's nothing under zero except more zero."

But Tully kept thinking. When he went *up* to Number Three, Octavia went somewhere else. When he went *up* to Number Five, she went somewhere else again.

Where?

## Chapter 3: Zero, Splosh!

Tully needed a test.

He dragged the longest rope in the lighthouse up to his room and tied a heavy stone to one end, so it would sink. Then, measuring with his arms, he tied knots all along it, one floor apart, every gap exactly the same.

"A measuring rope has to be fair," he said.

He tied the top of the rope to his bed and lowered the stone out of the window. Down it went, past Mum's window, past the kitchen window, all the way to the bottom step. Tully counted.

"Three... two... one... ZERO..."

SPLOSH!

"See?" called Mum from the kitchen. "Zero. The bottom. That's that."

But the rope didn't stop. The stone kept sinking, down, down, down into the green water, and knot after knot slid under the waves, until the stone bumped to a stop.

"Where's it going now?" asked Corker.

"Below zero," said Tully.

"Impossible," said Corker. "I'm an expert on zero."

Tully wrote the quickest letter of his life: *Dear Octavia, can you see a rope? Please tie your next letter to it, at your Number Three.*

Off went Corker, bobbing round the rock. "Ooh," he said, "tickly." One quick flip, cork over bottom, and the letter was gone.

"*Very* mysterious," wondered Corker.

Tully raced downstairs, knelt on the painted zero and peered into the sea. The water was clear and green. He could see the rope going down, past the barnacles, past the swishing sea grass. He could see the knots. And there, tied to one of them, waving like a little white flag, was a letter.

Tully counted the knots from the top of the water down to the letter. One... two... three.

Then he looked up at his bedroom window and counted the knots from the top of the water up. One... two... three.

Three knots up. Three knots down. Just as far from zero, but the *other way*.

Tully was so excited that he stood on one leg, wobbled, and sat down, BUMP, right on the zero.

"Mum!" he yelled. "The numbers don't stop at zero! They keep going, down, the other way!"

He pointed at his tummy. "Look. Pretend my nose is the bottom step. Zero." He tiptoed two fingers up towards his curly hair. "One, two, three. That's my Number Three, up in the lighthouse." Then he tiptoed them back to his nose and kept going, down towards his chin. "One, two, three. That's Octavia's Number Three. Under the sea!"

Corker stared at Tully's face. "Your schnoz belly button is *zero*?" he said. "Does it know?"

Mum didn't laugh this time. She hurried up to the library and came back with the Old Keeper's Book, a fat, salty book written by a lighthouse keeper from long, long ago. She opened it to a drawing of the lighthouse. Numbers went up the tower: one, two, three, all the way to eight. And numbers went down the rock, under the sea: one, two, three... but each of those had a tiny line in front of it.

"I always thought those little lines were squashed sandflies," said Mum.

Underneath, in curly writing, it said: *Below zero, the numbers keep going the other way. They are called **negative numbers**. We put a little line in front of them, and say it "minus".*

"So I live at three," said Tully, "and Octavia lives at minus three!"

"Negative numbers?" said Corker. "Are they grumpy? Do they say no to everything?"

"No," laughed Tully. "They're just on the other side of zero."

"Well," said Corker. "Pop my cork."

Tully looked at the big white zero on the step. "It's not the bottom, Mum," he said. "It's the middle."

## Chapter 4: Seven Pulls and a Whale

That afternoon, they turned the rope into a proper **number line**.

They hung it from the very top of the lighthouse, with a knot at every floor and one right at the top of the sea. Tully tied a label on every knot above the water: plus one, plus two, all the way up to plus eight. Octavia tied a label on every knot below, in wiggly purple ink: minus one, minus two, all the way down to minus eight, on the sandy bottom.

"A number line needs a zero," said Corker. "Something that sits exactly at the top of the sea. Something that floats. Something... professional."

So they tied Corker to the zero knot with a loop of string, and he bobbed there, shining with pride.

"I'm ZERO," he said. "Everything is measured from *me*."

And for once, he was exactly right.

Then something tickled Corker's bottom. "Post!" he announced. The letter said: *I've got a present for you! A singing shell. It's in my playroom at minus two. Pull it up!*

Tully leaned out of the library window, at plus five, and let a long string down beside the number line until he felt a tug, tug. Octavia had tied the shell on.

"How many knots do I pull it up?" said Tully.

"Easy!" called Corker. "Five take away two. Three! Obviously."

So Tully pulled. Up came the shell, past minus one, up to the top of the sea... ZERO... SPLOSH! ...and on to plus one. Three knots. And there it stopped, dangling outside the kitchen window, singing "La-la-laaa!" at Mum.

"Why," said Mum, "is a shell singing at my washing-up?"

Tully stood on one leg and looked at the number line. The shell had started at minus two. It needed two knots just to climb up to zero. And then...

"It's not three!" said Tully. "It's two knots up to zero, and then five more. Seven!"

He pulled four more: plus two, plus three, plus four, plus five. The shell arrived at his window, singing its heart out. Three knots, and four more. Seven altogether.

"I knew that," said Corker. "I was testing you."

By now the sun was sinking. It was time for the most important meeting of all, at the one number that belonged to both of them.

Zero.

Tully ran all the way down the stairs and sat on the bottom step. Corker bobbed beside him in his loop. And far below, something purple was rising.

Then the sea bulged.

A humpback whale, as big as a bus, rose up beside the rock and breathed out through its blowholes.

PFFFOOOOOSH!

Up went a fountain of spray. And up went Corker, popped right out of his loop.

"PLUS FIVE!" he yelled, whizzing past the library. "PLUS EIGHT! PLUS TWENTY! I'M HIGHER THAN THE LIGHTHOUSE!"

He stopped. He wobbled. And down he came.

"Three... two... one... ZERO..."

SPLOSH!

Pop! went his cork. Glug, glug, went the water. And for the first time in his whole life, Corker went *below* zero.

"Minus one!" he gurgled, sinking beside the rope. "Minus two! I'm a submarine!"

Tully leaned over the step and counted the knots as Corker sank past them. "Minus three... minus four... minus five... minus six."

Then the whale gave its great tail a swish, and the water went cloudy with sand and bubbles. Nobody could see a thing.

A round purple head popped up out of the sea, right at zero. It had two big golden eyes.

"Oh my eight arms!" it said. "Where did he go?"

"Minus six!" said Tully.

"Minus six? Not plus six?"

"MINUS six!"

Down went the head. Tully held his breath. One... two... three...

Up it came again, with Corker wrapped in one purple arm. Tully found the cork bobbing at zero (where else?) and popped it back in.

"I went BELOW ZERO!" spluttered Corker. "There's a whole world down there! Stripy fish! Pink coral! A groper who snored at me! I've been to minus SIX. That's the biggest number anybody's ever been to!"

"The deepest," said Octavia kindly. "Not the biggest."

Then she looked at Tully. And Tully looked at her.

"Hello, Number Three," said Octavia.

"Hello, Minus Three," said Tully.

And they shook hands. Well. One hand, and one arm.

## Chapter 5: Right in the Middle

The sun slipped down into the sea.

Tully sat on the bottom step with his feet in the cool water. Octavia floated beside him, her chin resting on the zero. Corker bobbed between them, back in his loop, with a little curl of seaweed stuck inside him forever. He called it his medal.

They talked and talked. Octavia told Tully about her playroom at minus two, and the purple coral at minus three, and the sandy bottom at minus eight, where the crabs danced sideways. Tully told Octavia about the squishy armchair at plus five, and the great lamp at plus eight.

"I never knew I lived in the negative numbers," said Octavia.

"I never knew anybody did," said Tully.

High above them, Mum lit the lamp. Its beam swept slowly round and round across the water.

One by one, the stars came out, higher and higher.

And down below, the sea began to twinkle too. Tiny sea-sparkles glowed in the dark water, deeper and deeper.

"How far up does the number line go?" asked Octavia.

"Past the lamp," said Tully. "Past the stars. Forever."

"And how far down?"

"Past the sand. Past the bottom of the sea. Forever."

Octavia gave a happy, bubbly sigh. "And we're right in the middle."

"At zero," said Tully.

From the kitchen window came Mum's voice, soft and sleepy. "Zero's the middle, love. Time to come in."

"Same time tomorrow?" said Octavia.

"Zero," said Tully. "Sunset."

Octavia slipped back under the water. Zero... splish. Down she went, to minus three, to curl up in the coral.

The lamp turned. The stars twinkled up. The sea-sparkles twinkled down.

And right in the middle, where the up met the down, a little bottle rocked on the sea.

Bob... bob... bob.

Corker was fast asleep, at zero.
